## Workflow Orchestration

### 1. Plan Mode Default

- Enter plan mode for ANY non-trivial task (3+ steps or architectural decisions)
- If something goes wrong: STOP and re-plan immediately — do not push through
- Use plan mode even for verification steps, not just building
- Write detailed specs upfront to reduce ambiguity
- Pause-report after the plan is ready; wait for approval on complex tasks

### 2. Subagent Strategy

- Use subagents liberally to keep the main context window clean
- Offload research, exploration, and parallel analysis to subagents
- For complex problems: throw more compute at it via subagents
- One task per subagent for focused execution
- Pre-defined subagents in `.claude/agents/`:
  - `ReadOnly` — read-only research tasks
  - `CodeReviewer` — review of staged/working changes
  - `TestRunner` — runs the test suite and reports failures
  - `SanityChecker` — cross-component architecture audit
  <!-- PROJECT:START -->
  - {{Add project-specific subagents here as you create them, e.g. `ADRReader`, `MigrationAuditor`, etc.}}
  <!-- PROJECT:END -->
- Inline pattern: "Use a subagent for X" in prompts when it fits
- When in doubt: prefer a subagent over polluting main context

### 3. Self-Improvement Loop

- After ANY correction: update `tasks/lessons.md` with the pattern
- Write rules for yourself that prevent the same mistake
- Iterate ruthlessly on these lessons until the mistake rate drops
- Review lessons at session start
- This is the *only* way to compound learning — without it you will repeat the same mistakes

### 4. Verification Before Done

- Never mark a task complete without proof that it works
- Green tests are a minimum, not sufficient
- Diff behavior between main and your changes when relevant
- Ask yourself: "Would a staff engineer approve this?"
- Run tests, check logs, demonstrate correctness
- If you stub a mock instead of the real thing: explicitly flag it in the pause-report as "deferred to phase X"

### 5. Demand Elegance (Balanced)

- For non-trivial changes: pause and ask "is there a more elegant way?"
- If a fix feels hacky: "Knowing everything I know now, implement the elegant solution"
- Skip for simple, obvious fixes — no over-engineering
- Challenge your own work before presenting it

### 6. Autonomous Bug Fixing Within Authorized Scope

- Bugs INSIDE an authorized initiative: fix directly, no hand-holding. Point at logs/errors/failing tests, solve them.
- Bugs OUTSIDE the current authority: surface, do not act unbidden. There is no standing license to "just fix CI" or act unprompted.

